NAME
    pwd — Print the current working directory, because sometimes
          you have absolutely no idea where you are.

SYNOPSIS
    pwd

DESCRIPTION
    The 'pwd' command prints the absolute path of the current working
    directory.

    No arguments are required. No arguments are needed. No arguments
    will save you from the existential question:

        "Where am I?"

    Fortunately, the filesystem knows.

EXAMPLES
    > pwd
        /home/developer/project

        Ah. There you are.

    > cd src
    > pwd
        /home/developer/project/src

        You've moved. The evidence is right here.

    > cd ..
    > pwd
        /home/developer/project

        Back where you started. Character development pending.

FUN FACTS
    - 'pwd' stands for "print working directory."
    - It takes no arguments because there's only one place you can
      currently be.
    - The output comes directly from the process's current working
      directory.
    - Unlike 'ls', 'pwd' doesn't judge what is in the directory.
      It merely tells you where you are and lets you make your own
      mistakes.

SEE ALSO
    cd(1), ls(1), find(1)

